3
00:00:10.265 --> 00:00:17.924
Programming begins with precise problem
statements, input and output examples, and

4
00:00:17.924 --> 00:00:21.406
algorithms written independently of syntax.

5
00:00:21.406 --> 00:00:29.440
Consider this worked case: A problem statement
defines what the program must do, while input

6
00:00:29.440 --> 00:00:34.796
and output examples specify the expected
behavior for specific cases.

7
00:00:34.796 --> 00:00:41.807
Trace each intermediate state in the example,
connect it to the formal claim, and verify

8
00:00:41.807 --> 00:00:45.546
that the final result satisfies the stated
condition.

9
00:00:45.546 --> 00:00:52.546
List the givens before choosing a method,
because an unstated assumption can change the

10
00:00:52.546 --> 00:00:53.046
conclusion.

11
00:00:53.046 --> 00:00:59.140
This establishes the vocabulary and scope
needed for the remaining sections.

12
00:00:59.258 --> 00:01:06.727
Now define Variables and State precisely
enough that another learner could test the

13
00:01:06.727 --> 00:01:07.301
