26
00:02:08.592 --> 00:02:16.638
Functions decompose a solution into named
operations with parameters and results.

27
00:02:16.638 --> 00:02:23.623
Consider this worked case: A function takes
input parameters, performs a specific

28
00:02:23.623 --> 00:02:30.607
operation, and returns a result, breaking down
complex problems into manageable parts.

29
00:02:30.607 --> 00:02:37.812
Trace each intermediate state in the example,
connect it to the formal claim, and verify

30
00:02:37.812 --> 00:02:41.654
that the final result satisfies the stated
condition.

31
00:02:41.654 --> 00:02:49.029
Follow every arrow in order and name the state
before and after each transition.

32
00:02:49.029 --> 00:02:54.435
The trace converts an abstract statement into
an inspectable sequence.

33
00:02:54.554 --> 00:03:01.606
Next, compare the choices involved in
Recursion and Base Cases and make the decision

34
00:03:01.606 --> 00:03:02.613
criteria explicit.

35
00:03:02.613 --> 00:03:09.175
Recursion solves a problem through smaller
instances and requires a base case.

36
00:03:09.175 --> 00:03:14.862
Consider this worked case: A recursive
function calls itself with a smaller input

37
00:03:14.862 --> 00:03:21.425
until it reaches a base case, at which point
it stops and returns a value.

38
00:03:21.425 --> 00:03:28.711
Trace each intermediate state in the example,
connect it to the formal claim, and verify

39
00:03:28.711 --> 00:03:32.597
that the final result satisfies the stated
condition.

40
00:03:32.597 --> 00:03:39.682
Compare alternatives against the same
constraints so the evaluation does not move

41
00:03:39.682 --> 00:03:40.863
the goalposts.

42
00:03:40.863 --> 00:03:47.613
Using shared criteria turns a preference into
a defensible technical choice.

43
00:03:47.738 --> 00:03:55.014
Apply Lists and Data Structures to a worked
situation instead of treating it as an

44
00:03:55.014 --> 00:03:55.984
isolated definition.

45
00:03:55.984 --> 00:04:04.532
Lists represent ordered sequences, stacks use
last-in first-out access, queues use first-in

46
00:04:04.532 --> 00:04:11.656
first-out access, trees represent hierarchy,
and graphs represent connected entities.

47
00:04:11.656 --> 00:04:19.631
Consider this worked case: A list stores items
in order, a stack adds and removes items from

48
00:04:19.631 --> 00:04:28.075
the top, a queue adds items to the back and
removes from the front, a tree organizes items

49
00:04:28.075 --> 00:04:31.828
hierarchically, and a graph connects entities
with edges.

50
00:04:31.828 --> 00:04:38.982
Trace each intermediate state in the example,
connect it to the formal claim, and verify

51
00:04:38.982 --> 00:04:42.797
that the final result satisfies the stated
condition.

52
00:04:42.797 --> 00:04:50.531
Work from the concrete inputs to the result,
checking units, types, or invariants at each

53
00:04:50.531 --> 00:04:51.047
step.

54
00:04:51.047 --> 00:04:56.375
The worked case shows both how to apply the
method and how to check it.

55
00:04:56.498 --> 00:05:03.720
Examine the tradeoffs in Control Structures,
including what improves and what becomes more

56
00:05:03.720 --> 00:05:04.276
