57
00:05:04.276 --> 00:05:11.323
Expressions produce values, while statements
control execution and bindings.

58
00:05:11.323 --> 00:05:17.782
Consider this worked case: An if statement
executes a block of code if a condition is

59
00:05:17.782 --> 00:05:23.838
true, while a for loop repeats a block of code
a specified number of times.

60
00:05:23.838 --> 00:05:31.001
Trace each intermediate state in the example,
connect it to the formal claim, and verify

61
00:05:31.001 --> 00:05:34.822
that the final result satisfies the stated
condition.

62
00:05:34.822 --> 00:05:41.885
State the benefit, the cost, and the condition
under which the tradeoff is acceptable.

63
00:05:41.885 --> 00:05:49.385
Making the tradeoff visible prevents a local
improvement from hiding a larger cost.

64
00:05:49.490 --> 00:05:57.213
Stress-test the reasoning around Search and
Sort Algorithms with an edge case and identify

65
00:05:57.213 --> 00:05:59.971
which assumption controls the outcome.

66
00:05:59.971 --> 00:06:06.393
Searching and sorting algorithms depend on
representation and invariants.

67
00:06:06.393 --> 00:06:14.946
Consider this worked case: A search algorithm
finds a specific item in a list, while a sort

68
00:06:14.946 --> 00:06:21.486
algorithm arranges items in order, both
relying on the data structure used and

69
00:06:21.486 --> 00:06:24.002
specific invariants to maintain correctness.

70
00:06:24.002 --> 00:06:31.135
Trace each intermediate state in the example,
connect it to the formal claim, and verify

71
00:06:31.135 --> 00:06:34.939
that the final result satisfies the stated
condition.

72
00:06:34.939 --> 00:06:41.268
Change one assumption at a time and observe
which part of the reasoning no longer holds.

73
00:06:41.268 --> 00:06:48.096
The edge case reveals the boundary of the
claim without discarding the useful core idea.

74
00:06:48.218 --> 00:06:56.421
Use Correctness and Testing to diagnose a
failure by separating symptoms, causes, and

75
00:06:56.421 --> 00:06:57.683
